\section{Deterministic Kernel Execution Model}

Let $\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE}}$ denote the set of TSSE kernels:
\[
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE}} =
\left\{
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE-T}},\;
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE-RPK}},\;
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE-FK}},\;
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE-SIK}},\;
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE-VK}},\;
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE-SK}},\;
\mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE-CK}}
\right\}.
\]

Define the deterministic kernel execution operator:
\[
\mathcal{E}_{\text{TSSE}} : \mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE}} \to \mathcal{K}_{\text{TSSE}},
\]
which executes kernels in a deterministic order.

\begin{definition}[Deterministic Kernel Execution]
A kernel execution operator $\mathcal{E}_{\text{TSSE}}$ is deterministic if:
\[
\mathcal{E}_{\text{TSSE}}(\mathcal{K}_i)
=
\mathcal{K}_{i+1},
\]
for all kernels in the TSSE hierarchy.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Execution Order Preservation]
If $\mathcal{E}_{\text{TSSE}}$ is deterministic, then kernel execution order is
preserved under TSSE evolution.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Deterministic execution defines a strict ordering; TSSE evolution preserves ordering
because kernels commute with $\mathcal{U}(t)$.
\end{proof}

This lemma establishes the deterministic nature of TSSE kernel execution.

