\section{Symplectic Numerical Integration}

Let $(q,p)$ denote canonical coordinates. TSSE requires that numerical integration
be symplectic:
\[
\Phi_{\Delta t}^\top J\,\Phi_{\Delta t} = J,
\]
where $\Phi_{\Delta t}$ is the discrete evolution operator.

\begin{definition}[Symplectic Integrator]
A numerical integrator $\Phi_{\Delta t}$ is symplectic if it preserves the canonical
symplectic form.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Symplectic Discretization]
If $\Phi_{\Delta t}$ is symplectic, then:
\[
\det(D\Phi_{\Delta t}) = 1.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Symplectic matrices have unit determinant.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Invariant-Preserving Numerical Evolution]
If $\Phi_{\Delta t}$ is symplectic and Hamiltonian-consistent, then:
\[
\mathcal{I}_{\text{TSSE}}(\Phi_{\Delta t}(q,p))
=
\mathcal{I}_{\text{TSSE}}(q,p).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Symplecticity preserves canonical structure; Hamiltonian consistency preserves
energy; temporal symmetry enforcement preserves $\mathcal{T}$ invariants.
\end{proof}

This theorem establishes invariant-preserving numerical evolution.

